## Feature #2 Increment 2 (spec §4.2) — `Scope#drop` cancels a still-pending
## child. A child that NEVER completes is spawned into a scope and is NOT
## collected; when the scope block exits, `Scope#drop` must cancel every
## still-pending child — firing its future's `def drop` via the §4.1 path
## (`ruxen_executor_cancel_task` → `Drop_dynamic_drop`) and removing its
## queue entry. So a detached/leaked task is unrepresentable: leaving the
## block cleans up the child deterministically.
##
## The `Leaker` sub-future stays Pending forever and has an OBSERVABLE
## `def drop`. We spawn it WITHOUT collecting (the block just spawns and
## returns), so at scope exit `Scope#drop` cancels it → "Leaker dropped"
## prints. Per-scope cancel: only THIS scope's child is cancelled (the
## thread queue isn't drained wholesale).

class Leaker
  payload: Int
  include Future
  type Output = Int

  def init(p: Int)
    self.payload = p
  end

  def self.make(p: Int) -> Leaker
    Leaker.new(p)
  end

  def var poll(cx: &var Context) -> Poll[Int]
    # Never completes — exists only to be cancelled by Scope#drop.
    Poll.Pending
  end

  def drop
    puts "Leaker dropped payload=#{self.payload}"
  end
end

def main
  puts "scope start"
  Scope.open do |s|
    # Spawn a never-completing child and DO NOT collect it. The scope owns
    # it; falling off the block must cancel it via Scope#drop.
    s.spawn(Leaker.make(42))
  end
  puts "scope done"
end
